  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... A hot and humid day out there today as temps have \ngotten into the mid 80s to low 90s this afternoon under partly \ncloudy skies with heat indices up around 100F. Some mid level dry \nair and subsidence aloft has kept the area precip free this \nafternoon for now, however, can't rule out a very isolated shower \nalong the sea breeze today. Not much relief from the heat tonight \nwith lows in the 70s. We could see some increasing cloud cover and \nmaybe some coastal showers/thunderstorms late tonight into Sun \nmorning as a weak mid level disturbance that is currently producing \nshower and thunderstorm activity in coastal SC will track north and \neast making its closest point of approach today. Will note there is \na better than not chance at much if not all of of this activity \nstaying offshore but kept SChc to low end Chc PoPs noted along the \nCrystal Coast and S'rn OBX to account for this threat.\n\nAs we get later into Sun and then next week upper ridging and \nBermuda high become suppressed as shortwave energy pushes across the \nMid-Atlantic with more of a zonal pattern developing next week with \na series of shortwave troughs pushing across the area. Next \npotential trough that makes its way slightly further south looks to \nbe around mid to late week timeframe with the typical uncertainty in \ntiming for this to occur. As a result climo precip chances are \nforecast for the next several days with best chances through peak \nheating and seabreeze related fro the most part/ Though chances and \ntiming for precip later in the week will also depend of timing and \nexact location of individual shortwave troughs. The severe threat \nappears minimal through much of the period with limited shear, \nthough somewhat better shear develops toward the middle of next \nweek. \n\nHot temperatures continue into next week as well with highs in the \nlower 90s inland into Monday, then warming in to the mid 90s Tue/Wed \nand beyond. Temps generally in the mid to upper 80s along the coast. \nThe probabilities for Major Heat Risk increase to around 50-80% \nMonday through Thursday with highest probs on Tuesday and Wednesday.",
